#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084269} :activity/id #uuid "b5963314-027e-58a8-94c3-2b38175169a4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050198 :topic/id #uuid "35c03b2e-e860-5cb2-96d6-2e103a19283f" :topic/math-academy-id 788} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084482 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ is normally distributed. Then the probability density function $f(x)$ of $X$ is a unimodal, bell-shaped curve. Moreover, it is symmetrical about the mean, median, and mode, which all equal $\\mu$.  \n\n![](images/0c/0c8951446f52cea9db6c0a2b600fc53cb564c3d64ff0afab15c9756dc0c0f29b.png)\n\nWe can use the normal distribution to model a wide range of real-life situations because many real-world distributions are roughly bell-shaped and symmetrical around a mean value. \n\nPatients' blood pressures, people's weights, measurement errors, and IQ scores are just some of the real-world examples that can be modeled as a normal distribution. \n\nTo determine whether a given data is normal, the first thing to do is to check the shape of the distribution. For example, if a data set is roughly bell-shaped, then it *might* be normally distributed. \n\nIt should be noted that, in practice, statisticians will often need to perform several tests on the data to check for normality (these are called **normality tests**). We cannot rely only on a distribution's shape since other distributions are also bell-shaped. However, checking the shape is often a good starting point." :tutorial/id #uuid "9f150bce-c1b4-502c-bcb1-da067550b958" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4966 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "932c4862-d05e-5b90-85c8-d54c3a5130a8" :step/math-academy-id 30927 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083126 :question/id #uuid "cdd74ee4-b82f